# Stress-testing a discontinuity
next: {"title":"Building a counterfactual state: synthetic control and Proposition 99","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/building-a-counterfactual-state-synthetic-control-and-proposition-99.cast"}

## Three Ways an RD Can Break
voice: male
level: advanced
use: rd_plot
with:
    jump: 2
    slope: 0.22
    curvature: 0.006
    noise: 0.3
    x_label: Age (running variable)
    cutoff_label: 65 — Medicare eligibility
    title: The jump at 65

Over the next four parts we'll take the regression discontinuity we built at age sixty-five and try, deliberately, to break it — with bandwidth, with manipulation, and with fuzziness.
    draw axes title

Each dot is an average outcome Y for people in a narrow band of age; the running variable along the bottom is simply how old you are.
    draw dots_left dots_right

At sixty-five Medicare eligibility switches on: roughly one in eight adults just under sixty-five is uninsured, and just past that line almost nobody is.
    draw cutoff_line

Before I fit anything — what licenses us to call the gap at that line causal, rather than age and health merely drifting together?

    pause 1.8

Nothing else about you changes discontinuously on your birthday, so the smooth part of the age–outcome relationship is soaked up by these two fits, one on each side.
    draw fit_left fit_right

What the fits cannot explain is the vertical gap between them exactly at sixty-five — and that gap is the estimate, an average effect that lives only right at the cutoff.
    draw jump_bracket

    pause 0.5

First stress test: that estimate lives at sixty-five, but it is fitted from data reaching away from sixty-five — so how far away should we let it reach?
    focus dots_left dots_right

    pause 1.5

Here is the danger — if the true relationship curves, a straight line fitted over a wide window tilts to chase the distant points, and that tilt lands inside the gap.
    animate curvature 0.03 duration 3

Narrow the window and the bias shrinks while the noise grows; widen it and you buy precision with bias — part two plots the estimate at every bandwidth at once, and a credible design is flat across a broad middle range.
    highlight jump_bracket effect glow

    quiz "Why does widening the bandwidth change the estimate?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Wider windows trade variance for bias — curvature far from the cutoff leaks into the measured jump.
        wrong: More data does cut noise, but it is the distant points that bend the fitted line.
        * More data means less bias
        + It pulls in points far from 65, where a straight fit must approximate a curved relationship
        * It moves the cutoff itself

Second stress test. The whole design assumes that whoever sits just below the line is a fair stand-in for whoever sits just above it.
    animate curvature 0.006 duration 1.5

So what would wreck that, if people could see the cutoff coming?

    pause 1.8

They would sort — and McCrary's density test, standard since 2008, ignores the outcome entirely and simply asks whether the count of observations itself jumps at the line.
    camera center.ref cutoff_line zoom 1.8

A pile-up just past the threshold is the fingerprint of sorting; age passes by construction, since nobody chooses their date of birth, while reported income next to a Medicaid eligibility threshold is exactly what a household can shade.
    camera reset true

    quiz "Which running variable is vulnerable to manipulation?"
        right: Income can be timed, reported or shifted; a birthday cannot, which is why the age-65 design is unusually safe here.
        wrong: Birthdays are fixed before anyone knows the rule — it is income that people can move across a threshold.
        * Age at your 65th birthday
        + Reported income relative to a Medicaid eligibility threshold

Third stress test, and the Oregon question again: does crossing the line actually make D equal one?
    highlight cutoff_line effect glow

    pause 1.8

In the lottery about thirty thousand names were drawn and only around a quarter ended up enrolled — and whenever crossing raises the probability of treatment part-way rather than all the way, the jump you see in Y shrinks in exactly that proportion.
    animate jump 0.9 duration 3

So the fuzzy design divides: the jump in Y over the jump in the probability of D — a Wald ratio, which is instrumental variables with 'past the cutoff' as the instrument.
    point at.ref jump_bracket gesture underline

And by the LATE theorem, that ratio is not the effect for everyone turning sixty-five; it is the effect for compliers at the cutoff — those who take up coverage because they crossed it.
    mark tau_mark target jump_bracket kind circle

    quiz "In a fuzzy RD, the jump in Y is divided by what?"
        right: You divide by the first-stage jump in D — the Wald ratio, delivering the LATE for compliers at the cutoff.
        wrong: Bandwidth and sample size affect precision; the denominator is the first-stage jump in treatment probability.
        * The bandwidth
        + The jump in the probability of treatment at the cutoff
        * The sample size

One jump, three ways to break it: the window you choose, the people who choose their side, and the gap between eligibility and take-up.
    delivery grave

## How Wide a Window?
voice: male
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,7]}
use: rd_plot
with:
    jump: 3.4
    slope: 0.18
    curvature: 0.026
    noise: 0.3
    x_label: Age
    cutoff_label: Age 65
    title: Any doctor visit, by age

    axes sens_axes x_label "Bandwidth h (years either side of 65)" y_label "Estimated jump (pp)" hidden true
    curve ci_hi dashed #8f887c expr "3.4 - 0.02*x^2 + 2.6/sqrt(x)" x_from 1 x_to 10 hidden true
    curve ci_lo dashed #8f887c expr "3.4 - 0.02*x^2 - 2.6/sqrt(x)" x_from 1 x_to 10 hidden true
    region ci_band x_from 1 x_to 10 between ["ci_hi","ci_lo"] style.fill #8f887c style.opacity 0.18 hidden true

Now that we have seen the three ways an RD can break, here is a fourth threat that is entirely of our own making: how wide a window we look at around the cutoff.
    draw axes title dots_left dots_right

Running variable is age, the cutoff is the sixty-fifth birthday, and each dot is the share of people in an age bin reporting a doctor visit.
    draw cutoff_line

    pause 0.3

Fit a line on each side, and the gap at the birthday is our estimate — about three and a half percentage points.
    draw fit_left fit_right jump_bracket

So here is the question: why should that single number change at all when I include fifty-five-year-olds instead of stopping at sixty-two?

    pause 1.8

Shrink the window and only the birthdays nearest the cutoff count — the most comparable people alive, but so few of them that the two fitted lines wobble with every stray dot.
    camera center.ref cutoff_line zoom 2.2

Widen it and every dot enters the regression: far more precision, but now the distant points come from a curved age profile, and a straight line through a curve leaves a gap at sixty-five that no treatment produced.
    camera reset true

Bias from curvature at one end, variance from thin data at the other — the bandwidth is simply a dial between those two errors.
    highlight fit_left fit_right effect glow

    quiz "As you widen the bandwidth, what happens?"
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: More data tightens the confidence interval, but the distant, curved points let curvature masquerade as a jump.
        wrong: Extra observations always buy precision — it is bias that a wider window buys you.
        * Bias falls, variance rises
        + Bias rises, variance falls
        * Both fall together

So a single estimate is not an answer. The honest thing is to show the whole dial.
    clear

    draw sens_axes

Bandwidth on the horizontal axis, in years either side of sixty-five; this line is the estimate you would have reported at each one.
    curve est_curve #2f6b8f expr "3.4 - 0.02*x^2" x_from 1 x_to 10
    label label_est "Estimate at each h" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to est_curve

    pause 0.3

Around it, the ninety-five percent confidence interval: enormous at a bandwidth of one year, and tightening steadily as the window grows.
    draw ci_hi ci_lo ci_band

The band keeps narrowing all the way to the right. So why not simply take the widest window and enjoy the precision?

    pause 1.8

Because the line beneath it has walked away: at nine years the estimate has fallen to one point eight, tightly estimated and quietly wrong.
    dot p_wide #8f887c at.x 9 at.y 1.78
    label label_wide "h = 9: precise, and drifting" below-left #8f887c attach_to p_wide
    delivery grave

At the other end the estimate is honest but the band is so wide that almost any policy story fits inside it.
    dot p_narrow #8f887c at.x 1 at.y 3.38
    label label_narrow "h = 1: right, but useless" above-right #8f887c attach_to p_narrow

    pause 0.5

The MSE-optimal bandwidth sits where squared bias and variance balance — here about four years — and what reassures us is not that one dot, but the flat stretch from one to five where the estimate barely moves.
    dot h_opt #b5482e at.x 4 at.y 3.08 guides true
    label label_hopt "h* ≈ 4 years (MSE-optimal)" above-right #b5482e attach_to h_opt
    mark mark_opt target label_hopt kind box

One catch worth carrying: that optimal width minimizes error in the point estimate, not in the interval around it — which is why Calonico, Cattaneo and Titiunik's robust bias-corrected intervals are now the standard report.
    highlight h_opt effect glow

    quiz "Your plot is flat from h = 1 to 5, then slides steeply downward from 6 onward. What do you conclude?"
        right: Stability near the cutoff is the reassurance, and the far drift is exactly the curvature bias the plot is built to expose.
        wrong: A drift only at wide bandwidths is the expected signature of curvature, not a broken design.
        * The design is broken
        + Local estimate is stable; the drift is curvature at wide windows
        * Report h = 9 — it is the most precise

A discontinuity estimate is a function of the window you chose — so report the function, not the number.
    delivery grave

## Who Chooses Which Side?
voice: male
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}

    axes ax_age x_label "Age (years)" y_label "People in the sample" hidden true
    curve dens_age #2f6b8f expr "64 - 0.15*x" x_from 10 x_to 90 hidden true
    label label_dens_age "Density of the running variable" above #2f6b8f attach_to dens_age hidden true
    arrow cut_age dashed #8f887c from.x 50 from.y 0 to.x 50 to.y 86 hidden true
    label label_cut_age "65th birthday: D_i = 1" above attach_to cut_age hidden true
    axes ax_inc x_label "Reported income (% of the poverty line)" y_label Applicants hidden true
    curve bench dashed #8f887c expr "64 - 0.15*x" x_from 10 x_to 90 hidden true
    label label_bench "No sorting: smooth" above-left #8f887c attach_to bench hidden true
    arrow cut_inc dashed #8f887c from.x 50 from.y 0 to.x 50 to.y 95 hidden true
    label label_cut_inc "138% of the poverty line" above attach_to cut_inc hidden true
    curve dens_left #b5482e expr "64 - 0.15*x + 26*exp(-0.02*(x-50)*(x-50))" x_from 10 x_to 50 hidden true
    label label_pile "Excess mass just below" above-left #b5482e attach_to dens_left hidden true
    curve dens_right #b5482e expr "64 - 0.15*x - 16*exp(-0.02*(x-50)*(x-50))" x_from 50 x_to 90 hidden true
    label label_hole "Missing mass just above" below-right #b5482e attach_to dens_right hidden true
    decision crit "Can you move your own X across the cutoff?" hidden true
    box birth "A birthday" #2f6b8f hidden true
    box inc "A reported income" #b5482e hidden true
    edge e_birth crit -> birth hidden true
    edge e_inc crit -> inc hidden true
    label lab_birth "Fixed at birth — no sorting possible" below #2f6b8f attach_to birth hidden true
    label lab_inc "Chosen, reported, and worth money" below #b5482e attach_to inc hidden true

Now that we have seen how the bandwidth changes the estimate, here is a threat no window can fix: who ends up on each side of the cutoff, and did they choose?
    draw ax_age

Forget the outcome for a moment — this curve just counts people at each age around 65, the running variable itself.
    draw dens_age label_dens_age

At the 65th birthday Medicare switches on, so D_i flips from zero to one exactly at that line.
    draw cut_age label_cut_age

So ask the question the whole design rests on: could anyone in this picture shift themselves from the left of the line to the right?

    pause 1.8

Nobody can — a birthday was fixed decades before the policy existed, so the density walks straight through the cutoff with no step in it.
    highlight dens_age effect glow

    clear

Change only the running variable: Medicaid eligibility, which turns on at 138 percent of the federal poverty line, where an unsorted count of applicants would glide through like this.
    draw ax_inc bench label_bench

But this cutoff is worth real money — a year of coverage — and every applicant knows exactly where it sits.
    draw cut_inc label_cut_inc

What should the histogram of reported income look like if households can nudge their number by a few hundred dollars?

    pause 1.8

A pile-up right under the line: far more applicants there than any smooth density would put there.
    draw dens_left label_pile

And it comes out of a hole just above — the same households, moved.
    draw dens_right label_hole

That step is what McCrary's 2008 density test looks for: fit the density separately on each side and ask whether the jump at the cutoff is distinguishable from zero.
    point at.x 50 at.y 60 gesture circle

It never touches the outcome — it asks only whether people sorted, because if they did, those just below differ systematically from those just above.
    highlight dens_left dens_right effect pulse

    quiz "A density with a spike just below the cutoff and a dip just above suggests what?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Excess mass below and missing mass above means people moved themselves across the line, so the two sides are no longer comparable.
        wrong: The density test says nothing about the size of the effect — it is about who ended up where.
        * The treatment effect is large
        + People sorted across the cutoff
        * The bandwidth is too narrow

    clear

The test is mechanical, but the judgement is not — before running anything, ask this of your running variable.
    draw crit

A birthday answers no: your date of birth was set long before anyone wrote the rule, which is why that density was smooth by construction.
    draw e_birth birth lab_birth

A reported income answers yes: self-reported, adjustable through hours or timing, and worth a year of coverage on the right side of the line.
    draw e_inc inc lab_inc

Manipulation needs all three at once — knowing where the line is, controlling your own value, and a payoff for crossing it.
    text verdict "Knowledge of the line + control of your value + a payoff" x 500 y 95 font_size 24
    mark mark_verdict target verdict kind box

And a failed test is not always cheating: birth weights bunch at round numbers like 1500 grams, pure measurement heaping — while a smooth density is necessary, never sufficient, so it belongs beside covariate balance checks across the cutoff.

    quiz "Which running variable is most vulnerable to manipulation?"
        right: Self-reported income is the one applicants can both observe and adjust, with money waiting on the other side.
        wrong: Ask which value the individual can both see and change — a birthday and a lottery draw are fixed for you.
        * Age at the 65th birthday
        + Self-reported income at an eligibility line
        * A lottery number drawn by the state

Regression discontinuity buys randomness only if nobody chose their side: Oregon's lottery earned that by design, a birthday earns it by biology, and a reported income never gets it for free.
    delivery grave

## Fuzzy RD, and What Survives
voice: male
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[55,75],"y":[0,1]}

    axes ax1 x_label Age y_label "Share with coverage (D)" hidden true
    curve d_left #2f6b8f expr "0.70 + 0.005*(x-55)" x_from 55 x_to 65 hidden true
    curve d_right #2f6b8f expr "0.95 + 0.004*(x-65)" x_from 65 x_to 75 hidden true
    dot dL_pt at.x 65 at.y 0.75 guides true hidden true
    dot dR_pt at.x 65 at.y 0.95 hidden true
    arrow d_gap dL_pt -> dR_pt #b5482e hidden true
    label lbl_d_gap "Jump in D = 0.20" right #b5482e attach_to d_gap hidden true
    axes ax2 x_label Age y_label "Share with a check-up (Y)" hidden true
    curve y_left #8a5fa8 expr "0.35 + 0.004*(x-55)" x_from 55 x_to 65 hidden true
    curve y_right #8a5fa8 expr "0.43 + 0.003*(x-65)" x_from 65 x_to 75 hidden true
    dot yL_pt at.x 65 at.y 0.39 guides true hidden true
    dot yR_pt at.x 65 at.y 0.43 hidden true
    arrow y_gap yL_pt -> yR_pt #b5482e hidden true
    label lbl_y_gap "Jump in Y = 0.04" right #b5482e attach_to y_gap hidden true
    text ratio_text "0.04 ÷ 0.20 = 0.20" #b5482e x 740 y 620 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark ratio_box target ratio_text kind box hidden true
    text iv1 "Crossing age 65" x 180 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text iv2 "Coverage D_i" #2f6b8f x 500 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text iv3 "Check-up Y_i" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    arrow a1 iv1 -> iv2 hidden true
    arrow a2 iv2 -> iv3 hidden true
    label lbl_a1 "+0.20 (first stage)" above #2f6b8f attach_to a1 hidden true
    label lbl_a2 "+0.20 for compliers" above #b5482e attach_to a2 hidden true

Now that we have seen who can choose their side of a cutoff, one crack is left: turning 65 does not actually insure everybody.
    draw ax1

Just below the birthday, about three quarters of this population hold some coverage, drifting up gently with age.
    draw d_left

On the birthday that share leaps to roughly ninety-five percent — high, but nowhere near everyone.
    draw d_right dL_pt dR_pt

The first stage is that leap: nought point two, one person in five moved into coverage by the calendar alone.
    draw d_gap lbl_d_gap

So if the cutoff changes treatment for only a fifth of people, how large should the jump in the outcome be?

    pause 1.8

    clear

Same age axis, now the outcome — the share who had a check-up in the past year.
    draw ax2

It trends smoothly with age, and at 65 it steps up too — but only a little.
    draw y_left y_right yL_pt yR_pt

Four percentage points. That is the reduced form: the effect of the birthday, diluted, because four fifths of these people had nothing about their coverage change.
    draw y_gap lbl_y_gap

Four points of outcome from twenty points of treatment — so what happened to the people the birthday actually insured?

    pause 1.6

Undo the dilution by dividing: nought point nought four over nought point two — a twenty-point effect on check-ups.
    draw ratio_text ratio_box delivery grave

    quiz "In this fuzzy design, what is being instrumented by what?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Crossing the cutoff is the instrument; actual coverage D_i is the endogenous treatment it shifts.
        wrong: Ask which variable the birthday moves first — it moves coverage, not the other way round.
        * Coverage instruments for age
        + Crossing the cutoff instruments for coverage
        * The outcome instruments for coverage

    quiz "Whose effect is that 0.20?"
        right: It is a LATE: the compliers at the cutoff, the fifth of people the birthday moved into coverage.
        * Everyone who turns 65
        * Everyone on Medicare
        + Those whose coverage the birthday itself changed

Oregon's lottery had exactly this shape: only about a quarter of the thirty thousand names drawn actually enrolled, and dividing the lottery's effect by that take-up gave the compliers' effect there too.
    highlight ratio_text effect glow

    clear

So a fuzzy RD is instrumental variables with a razor-thin instrument: the cutoff shifts coverage, coverage shifts care, and the ratio of the two jumps is the Wald estimate.
    draw iv1 a1 iv2 a2 iv3 lbl_a1 lbl_a2

What survives, then? First the window: an honest RD shows its estimate across many bandwidths instead of one lucky one.
    text t1 "1. Bandwidth: does the jump survive a narrower window?" x 500 y 430 font_size 26

Second the density: no pile-up just past the line — you cannot move your birthday, but you can certainly move your reported income.
    text t2 "2. Density: no pile-up on the lucky side of the line" x 500 y 355 font_size 26

Third, honesty about who is being measured: at a fuzzy cutoff, the compliers, and nobody else.
    text t3 "3. Fuzzy ratio: a LATE for compliers at the cutoff" x 500 y 280 font_size 26

The discontinuity itself was never the evidence — what you tried to break it with is.
    text t4 "An RD survives what you tried to break it with." #b5482e x 500 y 160 font_size 30
    mark ann_t4 target t4 kind box
    delivery grave

Cross a line and you get a causal effect: but only near the line, only for the people the line moved, and only if nobody chose which side to stand on.

